      <a href="#best-practices">Best Practices</a>
      <a href="#anti-patterns">Anti-Patterns</a>
    </nav>
  </div>

  <div class="hero">
    <h1>Writing Specs &amp; Tickets</h1>
    <p>An Agile guide to creating clear, actionable, and well-structured tickets — tailored to the DevTeam Hub ticket model.</p>
  </div>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <!-- Why it matters                                      -->
  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <div class="section">
    <h2><span class="icon">🎯</span> Why Good Tickets Matter</h2>
    <p>A well-written ticket is the <strong>contract between the product owner and the development team</strong>. It answers three questions:</p>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>What</strong> needs to be done?</li>
      <li><strong>Why</strong> does it matter?</li>
      <li><strong>How</strong> will we know it''s done?</li>
    </ol>
    <p>Poorly written tickets cause context switching, rework, scope creep, and frustration. Good tickets are the single best investment you can make in team velocity.</p>
  </div>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <!-- Anatomy of a Ticket                                 -->
  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <div class="section" id="anatomy">
    <h2><span class="icon">🧬</span> Anatomy of a Ticket</h2>
    <p>Every ticket in DevTeam Hub has these fields. Understanding each one ensures nothing is left ambiguous.</p>

    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Purpose</th><th>Tips</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>title</code></td><td>One-line summary of the work</td><td>Start with a verb. Be specific. Max ~80 chars.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>description</code></td><td>Full specification of the work</td><td>Include context, requirements, and acceptance criteria.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>kind</code></td><td>Type of work</td><td>story, meta_story, bug_fix, spike, hotfix</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>level</code></td><td>Complexity level</td><td>trivial, simple, moderate, complex, expert</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>status</code></td><td>Current workflow state</td><td>backlog → open → in_progress → in_review → testing → done</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>priority</code></td><td>Business urgency</td><td>low, medium, high, critical</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>story_points</code></td><td>Relative effort estimate</td><td>Fibonacci: 1, 2, 3, 5, 8, 13, 21</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>dev_estimate_hours</code></td><td>Developer time estimate</td><td>Hours of focused dev work</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>tester_estimate_hours</code></td><td>QA time estimate</td><td>Hours for testing + writing test cases</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>actual_hours</code></td><td>Time actually spent</td><td>Free-form: "2d 4h" or "16"</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>assignee</code></td><td>Developer responsible</td><td>Assign when sprint planning, not before.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>owner</code></td><td>Reporter / product owner</td><td>The person who can answer questions.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>sprint</code></td><td>Which sprint this belongs to</td><td>Only assign to current or next sprint.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>milestone</code></td><td>Release or feature group</td><td>Group related tickets for release tracking.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>how_to_reproduce</code></td><td>Bug reproduction steps</td><td>Required for bug_fix and hotfix tickets.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>test_plan</code></td><td>How to verify the work</td><td>Describe manual and automated test scenarios.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>tags</code></td><td>Labels for filtering</td><td>e.g. "performance", "auth", "frontend"</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>attachments</code></td><td>Supporting files</td><td>Screenshots, specs, design mockups.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <!-- Ticket Kinds                                        -->
  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <div class="section" id="kinds">
    <h2><span class="icon">🏷️</span> Ticket Kinds</h2>

    <h3>Story</h3>
    <p>A user-facing feature or capability. The most common ticket type. Always written from the user''s perspective.</p>
    <pre><code>As a [type of user], I want [goal], so that [benefit].</code></pre>

    <h3>Meta Story (Epic)</h3>
    <p>A large body of work that is broken down into multiple stories. Meta stories are <strong>never assigned directly</strong> — they track progress across child tickets.</p>

    <h3>Bug Fix</h3>
    <p>A defect in existing functionality. Must include <code>how_to_reproduce</code> steps. Priority should reflect user impact, not developer effort.</p>

    <h3>Hotfix</h3>
    <p>A critical production bug requiring immediate attention. Bypasses normal sprint flow. Branch pattern: <code>hotfix/T-&lt;id&gt;-&lt;slug&gt;</code>.</p>

    <h3>Spike</h3>
    <p>A time-boxed research task. The deliverable is <strong>knowledge, not code</strong>. Always set a time limit (e.g., 4h). The outcome should be documented in the ticket description.</p>

    <div class="tip">
      <strong>💡 Rule of thumb:</strong> If you can''t demo it, it''s a spike. If you can demo it, it''s a story.
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <!-- User Stories                                        -->
  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <div class="section" id="user-stories">
    <h2><span class="icon">📝</span> Writing User Stories</h2>

    <h3>The Format</h3>
    <pre><code>As a [persona],
I want [action/feature],
so that [value/outcome].</code></pre>

    <h3>The INVEST Criteria</h3>
    <p>Every good user story is:</p>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Letter</th><th>Criteria</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><strong>I</strong></td><td>Independent</td><td>Can be developed and delivered without depending on other stories.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>N</strong></td><td>Negotiable</td><td>Details can be discussed — it''s not a rigid contract.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>V</strong></td><td>Valuable</td><td>Delivers something meaningful to the user or business.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>E</strong></td><td>Estimable</td><td>The team can reasonably estimate the effort.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>S</strong></td><td>Small</td><td>Can be completed in one sprint (ideally 1–3 days of work).</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>T</strong></td><td>Testable</td><td>Has clear criteria to verify completion.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h3>Story Splitting Strategies</h3>
    <p>If a story is too large (>8 story points), split it using one of these approaches:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>By workflow step</strong> — Create, Read, Update, Delete as separate stories.</li>
      <li><strong>By business rule</strong> — Happy path first, then edge cases.</li>
      <li><strong>By data variation</strong> — Support one format first, then add others.</li>
      <li><strong>By interface</strong> — API first, then UI.</li>
      <li><strong>By operation</strong> — Simplest version first, then enhancements.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <!-- Acceptance Criteria                                 -->
  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <div class="section" id="acceptance">
    <h2><span class="icon">✅</span> Writing Acceptance Criteria</h2>
    <p>Acceptance criteria go in the <code>description</code> field. They define <strong>when the story is done</strong>.</p>

    <h3>Given-When-Then Format</h3>
    <pre><code>Given [precondition],
When  [action],
Then  [expected result].</code></pre>

    <h3>Checklist Format</h3>
    <p>For simpler stories, a checklist is acceptable:</p>
    <pre><code>Acceptance Criteria:
- [ ] User can see the login form on /login
- [ ] Email and password fields are validated
- [ ] Invalid credentials show an error message
- [ ] Successful login redirects to /dashboard
- [ ] Session persists across page reloads</code></pre>

    <div class="tip">
      <strong>💡 Tip:</strong> Acceptance criteria should be verifiable by QA without asking the developer. If a tester can''t verify it from the description alone, it''s not specific enough.
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <!-- Concrete Examples                                   -->
  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <div class="section" id="examples">
    <h2><span class="icon">📚</span> Examples Using Our Ticket Model</h2>

    <h3>Example 1: Story</h3>
    <div class="example-card">
      <h4>🎫 T-42: Add password reset flow to login page</h4>
      <div class="field"><span class="label">Kind:</span><span class="value">story</span></div>
      <div class="field"><span class="label">Level:</span><span class="value">moderate</span></div>
      <div class="field"><span class="label">Priority:</span><span class="value">high</span></div>
      <div class="field"><span class="label">Story Points:</span><span class="value">5</span></div>
      <div class="field"><span class="label">Dev Estimate:</span><span class="value">6h</span></div>
      <div class="field"><span class="label">QA Estimate:</span><span class="value">2h</span></div>
      <div class="field"><span class="label">Sprint:</span><span class="value">Sprint 12</span></div>
      <div class="field"><span class="label">Assignee:</span><span class="value">Jane Smith</span></div>
      <div class="field"><span class="label">Owner:</span><span class="value">Product Manager</span></div>
      <div class="field"><span class="label">Tags:</span><span class="value">auth, frontend</span></div>
      <div class="field"><span class="label">Description:</span></div>
      <pre><code>As a registered user,
I want to reset my password via email,
so that I can regain access to my account when I forget my password.

## Acceptance Criteria

Given I am on the login page,
When I click "Forgot password?",
Then I see a form asking for my email address.

Given I enter a valid registered email,
When I submit the form,
Then I receive a password reset email within 2 minutes.

Given I click the reset link in the email,
When I enter a new password (min 8 chars),
Then my password is updated and I am redirected to login.

Given I enter an unregistered email,
When I submit the form,
Then I see a generic "If this email exists..." message (no info leakage).

## Technical Notes
- Use Devise''s recoverable module
- Reset token expires after 2 hours
- Rate-limit to 3 requests per email per hour</code></pre>
      <div class="field"><span class="label">Test Plan:</span></div>
      <pre><code>1. Verify reset link appears on login page
2. Submit valid email → check email delivery
3. Submit invalid email → verify generic message (no enumeration)
4. Click reset link → change password → login with new password
5. Click expired link → verify error message
6. Try resetting 4 times → verify rate limit response</code></pre>
    </div>

    <h3>Example 2: Bug Fix</h3>
    <div class="example-card">
      <h4>🐛 T-67: Fix crash when uploading CSV with Unicode characters</h4>
      <div class="field"><span class="label">Kind:</span><span class="value">bug_fix</span></div>
      <div class="field"><span class="label">Level:</span><span class="value">simple</span></div>
      <div class="field"><span class="label">Priority:</span><span class="value">critical</span></div>
      <div class="field"><span class="label">Story Points:</span><span class="value">2</span></div>
      <div class="field"><span class="label">Dev Estimate:</span><span class="value">2h</span></div>
      <div class="field"><span class="label">QA Estimate:</span><span class="value">1h</span></div>
      <div class="field"><span class="label">Tags:</span><span class="value">import, encoding, backend</span></div>
      <div class="field"><span class="label">Description:</span></div>
      <pre><code>## Problem
Uploading a CSV file containing Hebrew or emoji characters causes a 500 error.
The import page crashes with an Encoding::InvalidByteSequenceError.

## Expected Behavior
CSV files with any valid UTF-8 content should import successfully.

## Impact
Blocks 3 customers who use Hebrew names in their data.</code></pre>
      <div class="field"><span class="label">How to Reproduce:</span></div>
      <pre><code>1. Go to /imports/new
2. Upload the attached file: test_hebrew.csv
3. Click "Import"
4. Observe: 500 error page
5. Expected: Import succeeds, 12 records created</code></pre>
      <div class="field"><span class="label">Test Plan:</span></div>
      <pre><code>1. Upload CSV with ASCII only → passes
2. Upload CSV with Hebrew characters → passes
3. Upload CSV with emoji → passes
4. Upload CSV with mixed encodings (Windows-1255) → graceful error message
5. Verify existing import specs still pass</code></pre>
    </div>

    <h3>Example 3: Spike</h3>
    <div class="example-card">
      <h4>🔬 T-88: Evaluate Redis vs Memcached for session storage</h4>
      <div class="field"><span class="label">Kind:</span><span class="value">spike</span></div>
      <div class="field"><span class="label">Level:</span><span class="value">moderate</span></div>
      <div class="field"><span class="label">Priority:</span><span class="value">medium</span></div>
      <div class="field"><span class="label">Story Points:</span><span class="value">3</span></div>
      <div class="field"><span class="label">Dev Estimate:</span><span class="value">4h (time-boxed)</span></div>
      <div class="field"><span class="label">QA Estimate:</span><span class="value">0h</span></div>
      <div class="field"><span class="label">Tags:</span><span class="value">infrastructure, performance</span></div>
      <div class="field"><span class="label">Description:</span></div>
      <pre><code>## Goal
Determine whether Redis or Memcached is better suited for our session
storage needs. Time-boxed to 4 hours.

## Questions to Answer
1. Which has lower latency for our session read pattern?
2. Which is easier to deploy with our current Docker setup?
3. What is the memory footprint for ~500 concurrent sessions?
4. Does Redis persistence add meaningful value for sessions?

## Deliverable
A summary in the ticket description with a recommendation and rationale.
No code changes expected.</code></pre>
    </div>

    <h3>Example 4: Hotfix</h3>
    <div class="example-card">
      <h4>🚨 T-91: Hotfix — Payment webhook returning 404 in production</h4>
      <div class="field"><span class="label">Kind:</span><span class="value">hotfix</span></div>
      <div class="field"><span class="label">Level:</span><span class="value">simple</span></div>
      <div class="field"><span class="label">Priority:</span><span class="value">critical</span></div>
      <div class="field"><span class="label">Story Points:</span><span class="value">1</span></div>
      <div class="field"><span class="label">Dev Estimate:</span><span class="value">1h</span></div>
      <div class="field"><span class="label">Tags:</span><span class="value">payments, production</span></div>
      <div class="field"><span class="label">Description:</span></div>
      <pre><code>## Incident
Production payment webhooks from Stripe are returning 404 since deploy v2.14.
Revenue impact: all new subscriptions are failing.

## Root Cause (suspected)
Route was renamed from /webhooks/stripe to /api/webhooks/stripe but
Stripe dashboard was not updated.

## Fix
Update routes.rb to accept both old and new paths.
Update Stripe dashboard webhook URL after deploy.</code></pre>
      <div class="field"><span class="label">How to Reproduce:</span></div>
      <pre><code>1. Send a test webhook from Stripe dashboard to /webhooks/stripe
2. Observe: 404 response
3. Send to /api/webhooks/stripe → 200 OK</code></pre>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <!-- Best Practices                                      -->
  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <div class="section" id="best-practices">
    <h2><span class="icon">⭐</span> Best Practices</h2>

    <h3>Writing Titles</h3>
    <div class="grid-2">
      <div class="tip">
        <strong>✅ Good titles:</strong>
        <ul>
          <li>Add password reset flow to login page</li>
          <li>Fix CSV import crash with Unicode characters</li>
          <li>Show sprint velocity chart on dashboard</li>
          <li>Evaluate Redis vs Memcached for sessions</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="bad">
        <strong>❌ Bad titles:</strong>
        <ul>
          <li>Login page</li>
          <li>Bug fix</li>
          <li>Improve performance</li>
          <li>Update stuff</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>

    <h3>Description Guidelines</h3>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Start with context</strong> — Why are we doing this? What''s the user problem?</li>
      <li><strong>Be specific</strong> — "Users report slow load times" → "Dashboard takes 8s to load with >100 tickets"</li>
      <li><strong>Include acceptance criteria</strong> — Every story needs them. No exceptions.</li>
      <li><strong>Add technical notes</strong> — Architecture decisions, API contracts, database changes.</li>
      <li><strong>Attach mockups</strong> — A screenshot or wireframe is worth a thousand words.</li>
    </ol>

    <h3>Workflow Best Practices</h3>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Status</th><th>Who moves it</th><th>When</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>backlog</code></td><td>Product Owner</td><td>When the ticket is created but not yet prioritized</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>open</code></td><td>Product Owner</td><td>After sprint planning — ready to be picked up</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>in_progress</code></td><td>Developer</td><td>When work begins (<code>dt ticket checkout</code> does this automatically)</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>in_review</code></td><td>Developer</td><td>When PR is submitted (<code>dt ticket commit</code> does this)</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>testing</code></td><td>Reviewer</td><td>After code review passes, QA begins testing</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>done</code></td><td>QA / Developer</td><td>When all acceptance criteria pass</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>blocked</code></td><td>Anyone</td><td>When external dependency prevents progress</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h3>Definition of Done (DoD)</h3>
    <p>A ticket is <strong>done</strong> when:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>All acceptance criteria are met</li>
      <li>Code has been reviewed and approved</li>
      <li>Automated tests are written and passing</li>
      <li>No regressions in existing test suite</li>
      <li><code>actual_hours</code> is filled in</li>
      <li>Documentation is updated (if applicable)</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Definition of Ready (DoR)</h3>
    <p>A ticket is <strong>ready for sprint</strong> when:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Title is clear and actionable</li>
      <li>Description includes acceptance criteria</li>
      <li>Story points are estimated</li>
      <li>Dependencies are identified and resolved</li>
      <li>Mockups/designs are attached (for UI work)</li>
      <li>Test plan is drafted</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <!-- Anti-Patterns                                       -->
  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <div class="section" id="anti-patterns">
    <h2><span class="icon">🚫</span> Common Anti-Patterns</h2>

    <div class="grid-2">
      <div class="bad">
        <strong>❌ The Vague Ticket</strong>
        <p>"Make the app faster" — No acceptance criteria, no definition of done, no way to know when you''re finished.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="tip">
        <strong>✅ Instead</strong>
        <p>"Reduce dashboard load time from 8s to under 2s for accounts with 100+ tickets. Measure with PageSpeed Insights."</p>
      </div>

      <div class="bad">
        <strong>❌ The Kitchen Sink</strong>
        <p>"Build the entire reporting module" — Too large to estimate, too complex for one sprint, impossible to review.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="tip">
        <strong>✅ Instead</strong>
        <p>Create a meta_story and split into: "Show ticket count by status chart", "Add CSV export for sprint report", etc.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="bad">
        <strong>❌ The Solution Disguised as a Story</strong>
        <p>"Add a Redis cache to the User model" — Describes implementation, not the user problem.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="tip">
        <strong>✅ Instead</strong>
        <p>"As a user, I want the profile page to load in under 1 second." Let the developer choose the implementation.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="bad">
        <strong>❌ The Missing Bug Report</strong>
        <p>"Login doesn''t work" — No reproduction steps, no expected vs actual behavior, no environment info.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="tip">
        <strong>✅ Instead</strong>
        <p>Fill in <code>how_to_reproduce</code> with numbered steps, browser/OS, and attach a screenshot.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <!-- Ticket Template                                     -->
  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <div class="section" id="templates">
    <h2><span class="icon">📄</span> Quick Templates</h2>

    <h3>Story Template</h3>
    <pre><code>Title: [Verb] [feature] [context]
Kind:  story
Level: [trivial|simple|moderate|complex|expert]

## User Story
As a [persona],
I want [action],
so that [benefit].

## Acceptance Criteria
- [ ] Criterion 1
- [ ] Criterion 2
- [ ] Criterion 3

## Technical Notes
- [Architecture decisions, API changes, DB migrations]

## Test Plan
1. [Test scenario 1]
2. [Test scenario 2]</code></pre>

    <h3>Bug Report Template</h3>
    <pre><code>Title: Fix [symptom] when [action]
Kind:  bug_fix

## Problem
[What''s broken and who is affected]

## Expected Behavior
[What should happen]

## How to Reproduce
1. Go to [page]
2. Do [action]
3. Observe: [actual result]
4. Expected: [expected result]

## Environment
- Browser: [Chrome 120 / Safari 17]
- OS: [macOS 14 / Windows 11]

## Test Plan
1. [Verify fix for reported case]
2. [Verify no regression]</code></pre>
  </div>

  <div class="footer">
    DevTeam Hub — Writing Specs &amp; Tickets Guide — May 2026
  </div>
</div>
</body>
</html>
