  <div class="hero">
    <h1>Ticket Estimation</h1>
    <p>A practical guide to estimating work in Agile — using story points, complexity levels, and hour estimates from the DevTeam Hub model.</p>
  </div>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <!-- Estimation Fields                                   -->
  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <div class="section" id="fields">
    <h2><span class="icon">🧮</span> Estimation Fields in DevTeam Hub</h2>
    <p>Our ticket model provides multiple estimation dimensions. Each serves a different purpose:</p>

    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Type</th><th>Purpose</th><th>Who Sets It</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><code>story_points</code></td>
          <td>Integer</td>
          <td>Relative effort/complexity. Used for sprint planning and velocity tracking.</td>
          <td>Team (during planning)</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>level</code></td>
          <td>Enum</td>
          <td>Complexity classification: trivial, simple, moderate, complex, expert.</td>
          <td>Tech lead or developer</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>dev_estimate_hours</code></td>
          <td>Decimal</td>
          <td>Estimated developer hours for implementation.</td>
          <td>Assigned developer</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>tester_estimate_hours</code></td>
          <td>Decimal</td>
          <td>Estimated QA hours for testing.</td>
          <td>QA engineer</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>actual_hours</code></td>
          <td>String</td>
          <td>Time actually spent (e.g. "2d 4h"). Recorded after completion.</td>
          <td>Developer</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>actual_velocity</code></td>
          <td>Integer</td>
          <td>Actual story points delivered (may differ from estimate).</td>
          <td>Team (during retro)</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>estimated_by</code></td>
          <td>User ref</td>
          <td>Who last updated the estimate. Auto-set on ticket update.</td>
          <td>System (automatic)</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <div class="tip">
      <strong>💡 Key insight:</strong> Story points measure <em>complexity</em>, not <em>time</em>. A 5-point story for a senior developer and a junior developer takes different hours — but the complexity is the same.
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <!-- Story Points                                        -->
  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <div class="section" id="story-points">
    <h2><span class="icon">🎯</span> Story Points — The Fibonacci Scale</h2>
    <p>We use the <strong>modified Fibonacci sequence</strong> for story points. The growing gaps between numbers force the team to acknowledge uncertainty in larger items.</p>

    <div class="grid-7">
      <div class="points-card">
        <div class="number">1</div>
        <div class="desc">Trivial change</div>
      </div>
      <div class="points-card">
        <div class="number">2</div>
        <div class="desc">Small &amp; clear</div>
      </div>
      <div class="points-card">
        <div class="number">3</div>
        <div class="desc">Moderate work</div>
      </div>
      <div class="points-card">
        <div class="number">5</div>
        <div class="desc">Significant effort</div>
      </div>
      <div class="points-card">
        <div class="number">8</div>
        <div class="desc">Large &amp; complex</div>
      </div>
      <div class="points-card">
        <div class="number">13</div>
        <div class="desc">Very large</div>
      </div>
      <div class="points-card">
        <div class="number">21</div>
        <div class="desc">Should split</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <h3>What Each Point Value Means</h3>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Points</th><th>Complexity</th><th>Typical Work</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>1</strong></td>
          <td>Trivial</td>
          <td>Config change, copy update, one-line fix</td>
          <td>Change button label from "Submit" to "Save"</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>2</strong></td>
          <td>Simple</td>
          <td>Well-understood, small scope, clear path</td>
          <td>Add a new column to an existing table, add validation</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>3</strong></td>
          <td>Moderate</td>
          <td>Standard feature, some design decisions</td>
          <td>Add email notification when ticket is assigned</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>5</strong></td>
          <td>Significant</td>
          <td>Multiple components, moderate uncertainty</td>
          <td>Build CSV export with filters, pagination, and download</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>8</strong></td>
          <td>Complex</td>
          <td>Cross-cutting concern, integration work, new patterns</td>
          <td>Integrate third-party payment gateway with webhooks</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>13</strong></td>
          <td>Very complex</td>
          <td>Multiple unknowns, architectural changes, high risk</td>
          <td>Migrate session storage from cookies to Redis cluster</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>21</strong></td>
          <td>Epic-sized</td>
          <td>⚠ Too large — must be split into smaller stories</td>
          <td>Build entire reporting module (split this!)</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <div class="warn">
      <strong>⚠ Rule:</strong> If a story is estimated at 13+ points, the team should discuss splitting it before committing it to a sprint. Stories above 8 points carry significant estimation risk.
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <!-- Complexity Levels                                   -->
  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <div class="section" id="complexity">
    <h2><span class="icon">⚙️</span> Complexity Levels</h2>
    <p>The <code>level</code> field classifies the technical complexity independently of effort. It helps with task assignment and load balancing.</p>

    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Level</th><th>Description</th><th>Who Should Work On It</th><th>Typical Points</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><code>trivial</code></td>
          <td>No design decisions. Clear, mechanical change.</td>
          <td>Any team member, including juniors</td>
          <td>1–2</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>simple</code></td>
          <td>Well-understood pattern. One component affected.</td>
          <td>Junior or mid-level developer</td>
          <td>2–3</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>moderate</code></td>
          <td>Some design choices. Multiple components involved.</td>
          <td>Mid-level developer</td>
          <td>3–5</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>complex</code></td>
          <td>Significant design decisions. New patterns or integrations.</td>
          <td>Senior developer</td>
          <td>5–8</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>expert</code></td>
          <td>Architectural impact. Deep domain or infrastructure knowledge.</td>
          <td>Senior / tech lead with domain expertise</td>
          <td>8–13</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h3>Level vs Points — They''re Different</h3>
    <div class="grid-2">
      <div class="tip">
        <strong>Level = Technical Difficulty</strong>
        <p>How hard is the problem? Does it require deep expertise? How many unknowns exist?</p>
      </div>
      <div class="tip">
        <strong>Points = Total Effort</strong>
        <p>How much work is involved? Includes implementation, testing, review, and deployment.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p>A <code>simple</code> level ticket can still be <strong>5 points</strong> if it involves many repetitive changes across files. A <code>complex</code> ticket might be only <strong>3 points</strong> if the solution is small but requires deep understanding.</p>
  </div>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <!-- Hour Estimates                                      -->
  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <div class="section" id="hours">
    <h2><span class="icon">⏰</span> Hour-Based Estimates</h2>
    <p>While story points measure complexity, <code>dev_estimate_hours</code> and <code>tester_estimate_hours</code> provide <strong>calendar-time forecasts</strong> for capacity planning.</p>

    <h3>Guideline: Points to Hours</h3>
    <p>The exact ratio depends on team velocity, but here''s a starting reference:</p>

    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Story Points</th><th>Dev Hours (approx)</th><th>QA Hours (approx)</th><th>Total</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>1</td><td>0.5–1h</td><td>0.5h</td><td>1–1.5h</td></tr>
        <tr><td>2</td><td>2–3h</td><td>1h</td><td>3–4h</td></tr>
        <tr><td>3</td><td>4–6h</td><td>1.5–2h</td><td>5.5–8h</td></tr>
        <tr><td>5</td><td>6–10h</td><td>2–3h</td><td>8–13h</td></tr>
        <tr><td>8</td><td>12–18h</td><td>3–5h</td><td>15–23h</td></tr>
        <tr><td>13</td><td>20–30h</td><td>5–8h</td><td>25–38h</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <div class="tip">
      <strong>💡 Tip:</strong> These are <em>focused work hours</em>, not wall-clock time. A developer with meetings and code reviews might have 5–6 focused hours in an 8-hour day.
    </div>

    <h3>Recording Actual Hours</h3>
    <p>The <code>actual_hours</code> field accepts flexible formats. DevTeam Hub parses them into hours (1 day = 8 hours):</p>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Input</th><th>Parsed As</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>2d 4h</code></td><td>20 hours (2 × 8 + 4)</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>5h</code></td><td>5 hours</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>1d</code></td><td>8 hours</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>16</code></td><td>16 hours</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>0.5d 2h</code></td><td>6 hours (0.5 × 8 + 2)</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <!-- Estimation Techniques                               -->
  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <div class="section" id="techniques">
    <h2><span class="icon">🃏</span> Estimation Techniques</h2>

    <h3>1. Planning Poker</h3>
    <p>The most common Agile estimation technique. Best for team-wide consensus.</p>
    <ol>
      <li>Product owner reads the ticket aloud</li>
      <li>Team discusses and asks clarifying questions</li>
      <li>Each member privately picks a Fibonacci card (1, 2, 3, 5, 8, 13, 21)</li>
      <li>All cards are revealed simultaneously</li>
      <li>If estimates differ by more than 2 steps, the highest and lowest explain their reasoning</li>
      <li>Re-vote until consensus (within 1 step)</li>
    </ol>

    <div class="tip">
      <strong>💡 Time-box:</strong> Spend maximum 5 minutes per ticket. If you can''t reach consensus, the ticket probably needs more refinement.
    </div>

    <h3>2. T-Shirt Sizing</h3>
    <p>Quick and informal. Good for backlog grooming when precise numbers aren''t needed yet.</p>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Size</th><th>Equivalent Points</th><th>Level</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>XS</td><td>1</td><td>trivial</td></tr>
        <tr><td>S</td><td>2–3</td><td>simple</td></tr>
        <tr><td>M</td><td>5</td><td>moderate</td></tr>
        <tr><td>L</td><td>8</td><td>complex</td></tr>
        <tr><td>XL</td><td>13+</td><td>expert (needs splitting)</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h3>3. Reference Story Comparison</h3>
    <p>Compare each new ticket to a "reference story" the team has already completed. This anchors estimates in reality.</p>
    <ol>
      <li>Pick 3 completed tickets as reference points (small, medium, large)</li>
      <li>For each new ticket, ask: "Is this bigger or smaller than our reference?"</li>
      <li>Assign points relative to the references</li>
    </ol>

    <h3>4. Three-Point Estimation (for hours)</h3>
    <p>When hour accuracy matters (client billing, deadline forecasting), use three estimates:</p>
    <div class="formula">
      <strong>Estimate</strong> = (Optimistic + 4 × Most Likely + Pessimistic) ÷ 6
    </div>
    <p>Example: A feature might take 4h (best case), 8h (most likely), or 20h (worst case):</p>
    <div class="formula">
      (4 + 4 × 8 + 20) ÷ 6 = <strong>9.3h</strong> → set <code>dev_estimate_hours</code> to <strong>9.5</strong>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <!-- Examples                                            -->
  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <div class="section" id="examples">
    <h2><span class="icon">📚</span> Estimation Examples</h2>

    <h3>Example 1: Simple Feature</h3>
    <div class="example-card">
      <h4>🎫 T-42: Add "Last Login" timestamp to user profile page</h4>
      <div class="field"><span class="label">Kind:</span><span class="value">story</span></div>
      <div class="field"><span class="label">Level:</span><span class="value">simple</span></div>
      <div class="field"><span class="label">Story Points:</span><span class="value">2</span></div>
      <div class="field"><span class="label">Dev Estimate:</span><span class="value">2h</span></div>
      <div class="field"><span class="label">QA Estimate:</span><span class="value">0.5h</span></div>
      <div class="field"><span class="label">Rationale:</span></div>
      <pre><code>- Migration: add last_login_at column (trivial)
- Model: update Devise callback to set timestamp (known pattern)
- View: display formatted date on profile (simple)
- Test: one model spec + one request spec
- No design decisions, no unknowns → level: simple, 2 points</code></pre>
      <div class="field"><span class="label">Actual Hours:</span><span class="value">1.5h (came in under estimate ✓)</span></div>
    </div>

    <h3>Example 2: Moderate Feature</h3>
    <div class="example-card">
      <h4>🎫 T-55: Add ticket comment notifications via email</h4>
      <div class="field"><span class="label">Kind:</span><span class="value">story</span></div>
      <div class="field"><span class="label">Level:</span><span class="value">moderate</span></div>
      <div class="field"><span class="label">Story Points:</span><span class="value">5</span></div>
      <div class="field"><span class="label">Dev Estimate:</span><span class="value">8h</span></div>
      <div class="field"><span class="label">QA Estimate:</span><span class="value">2h</span></div>
      <div class="field"><span class="label">Rationale:</span></div>
      <pre><code>- Mailer: new TicketMailer.comment_added (moderate)
- Job: background job to send emails (known pattern)
- Model: ticket_watchers notification preferences (some design)
- UI: "Watch this ticket" toggle on ticket show (small)
- Tests: mailer spec + job spec + request spec
- Some decisions needed:
  - Who gets notified? (assignee, owner, watchers)
  - How to handle reply-by-email? (punt to later)
- Multiple components but well-understood → 5 points</code></pre>
      <div class="field"><span class="label">Actual Hours:</span><span class="value">1d 2h (10h — slightly over due to email template styling)</span></div>
    </div>

    <h3>Example 3: Complex Integration</h3>
    <div class="example-card">
      <h4>🎫 T-73: Integrate Gitea webhooks for automatic PR sync</h4>
      <div class="field"><span class="label">Kind:</span><span class="value">story</span></div>
      <div class="field"><span class="label">Level:</span><span class="value">complex</span></div>
      <div class="field"><span class="label">Story Points:</span><span class="value">8</span></div>
      <div class="field"><span class="label">Dev Estimate:</span><span class="value">16h</span></div>
      <div class="field"><span class="label">QA Estimate:</span><span class="value">4h</span></div>
      <div class="field"><span class="label">Rationale:</span></div>
      <pre><code>- Webhook endpoint: receive and validate Gitea payloads (moderate)
- Payload parsing: handle PR opened/updated/merged events (moderate)
- PR model sync: create/update pull_request records (moderate)
- Ticket linking: match branch name to ticket ID (simple)
- Security: validate webhook signature, rate limiting (significant)
- Error handling: retries, logging, dead-letter queue (moderate)
- Tests: multiple edge cases, webhook payload fixtures
- Unknowns:
  - Gitea webhook payload format (need to research)
  - How to handle force-pushes and rebases
- Multiple unknowns + cross-cutting → level: complex, 8 points</code></pre>
      <div class="field"><span class="label">Actual Hours:</span><span class="value">2d 6h (22h — Gitea payload edge cases took longer)</span></div>
    </div>

    <h3>Example 4: Bug Fix</h3>
    <div class="example-card">
      <h4>🐛 T-81: Fix timezone display in sprint date picker</h4>
      <div class="field"><span class="label">Kind:</span><span class="value">bug_fix</span></div>
      <div class="field"><span class="label">Level:</span><span class="value">simple</span></div>
      <div class="field"><span class="label">Story Points:</span><span class="value">1</span></div>
      <div class="field"><span class="label">Dev Estimate:</span><span class="value">1h</span></div>
      <div class="field"><span class="label">QA Estimate:</span><span class="value">0.5h</span></div>
      <div class="field"><span class="label">Rationale:</span></div>
      <pre><code>- Root cause identified: date picker sends UTC, display assumes local
- Fix: add timezone conversion in date_helper.rb
- Risk: low (isolated change, well-tested area)
- 1 point for a focused, well-understood fix</code></pre>
      <div class="field"><span class="label">Actual Hours:</span><span class="value">1h ✓</span></div>
    </div>

    <h3>Example 5: Spike</h3>
    <div class="example-card">
      <h4>🔬 T-88: Spike — Evaluate full-text search options</h4>
      <div class="field"><span class="label">Kind:</span><span class="value">spike</span></div>
      <div class="field"><span class="label">Level:</span><span class="value">moderate</span></div>
      <div class="field"><span class="label">Story Points:</span><span class="value">3</span></div>
      <div class="field"><span class="label">Dev Estimate:</span><span class="value">4h (time-boxed)</span></div>
      <div class="field"><span class="label">QA Estimate:</span><span class="value">0h</span></div>
      <div class="field"><span class="label">Rationale:</span></div>
      <pre><code>- Research task: no code delivered
- Time-boxed to 4 hours regardless of outcome
- Deliverable: written recommendation in ticket description
- 3 points for research effort + documentation
- Always estimate spikes at the time-box duration</code></pre>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <!-- Tracking & Velocity                                 -->
  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <div class="section" id="tracking">
    <h2><span class="icon">📊</span> Tracking Actuals &amp; Velocity</h2>

    <h3>Why Track Actuals?</h3>
    <p>Comparing estimates to actuals over time reveals:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Estimation accuracy</strong> — Are we consistently over or under-estimating?</li>
      <li><strong>Team velocity</strong> — How many points can we reliably deliver per sprint?</li>
      <li><strong>Capacity planning</strong> — How much work can we commit to next sprint?</li>
      <li><strong>Process improvement</strong> — Which types of work have the widest estimate gaps?</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Velocity Formula</h3>
    <div class="formula">
      <strong>Sprint Velocity</strong> = Sum of <code>story_points</code> for all tickets marked <code>done</code> in the sprint
    </div>
    <p>Use the <strong>rolling average of the last 3 sprints</strong> for planning. Don''t use a single sprint — it''s too volatile.</p>

    <h3>Estimation Accuracy</h3>
    <div class="formula">
      <strong>Accuracy</strong> = <code>dev_estimate_hours</code> ÷ <code>actual_hours</code> × 100%
    </div>
    <p>Good teams aim for <strong>80–120% accuracy</strong>. Below 80% means systematic under-estimation. Above 120% means padding.</p>

    <h3>Using the CLI to Track</h3>
    <pre><code># Update actual hours after completing work
$ dt ticket update 42 actual_hours "1d 6h"

# View estimation accuracy in reports
# Reports → Estimation Accuracy (in the web UI)</code></pre>

    <div class="tip">
      <strong>💡 Best practice:</strong> Fill in <code>actual_hours</code> immediately when moving a ticket to <code>done</code>. Waiting until the end of the sprint leads to inaccurate numbers.
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <!-- Best Practices                                      -->
  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <div class="section" id="best-practices">
    <h2><span class="icon">⭐</span> Estimation Best Practices</h2>

    <ol>
      <li><strong>Estimate as a team</strong> — The collective wisdom of the team is more accurate than any individual. Use Planning Poker.</li>
      <li><strong>Estimate complexity, not duration</strong> — "How hard is this?" not "How long will it take you?"</li>
      <li><strong>Include everything</strong> — Implementation + tests + code review + documentation. Not just the fun coding part.</li>
      <li><strong>Use reference stories</strong> — Compare to past work the team has actually delivered.</li>
      <li><strong>Don''t estimate bugs at 0</strong> — Even a one-line fix needs investigation, testing, and review.</li>
      <li><strong>Time-box spikes</strong> — Always set a maximum time. The output is knowledge, not perfection.</li>
      <li><strong>Re-estimate when scope changes</strong> — If requirements change mid-sprint, update the estimates.</li>
      <li><strong>Track and learn</strong> — Review estimate accuracy in retrospectives. Adjust calibration over time.</li>
      <li><strong>Don''t game the numbers</strong> — Estimates are forecasts, not commitments. Padding destroys trust.</li>
      <li><strong>Split, don''t inflate</strong> — If a story feels too big, split it into smaller stories instead of giving it a huge number.</li>
    </ol>
  </div>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <!-- Pitfalls                                            -->
  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <div class="section" id="pitfalls">
    <h2><span class="icon">🚫</span> Common Estimation Pitfalls</h2>

    <div class="grid-2">
      <div class="bad">
        <strong>❌ Anchoring Bias</strong>
        <p>The first person to say a number influences everyone else. That''s why Planning Poker uses simultaneous reveal.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="bad">
        <strong>❌ Optimism Bias</strong>
        <p>"It''s just a simple change" — until you discover the test suite, the edge cases, and the code review feedback.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="bad">
        <strong>❌ Confusing Effort with Duration</strong>
        <p>An 8-point story isn''t "8 hours of work." It might be 16 hours spread across 3 days with interruptions.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="bad">
        <strong>❌ Not Including QA</strong>
        <p>A feature isn''t done when the code is written. Include <code>tester_estimate_hours</code> in sprint capacity.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="bad">
        <strong>❌ Hero Estimation</strong>
        <p>"I can do this in 2 hours" — but what if you''re sick? Estimate for the average team member, not the fastest.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="bad">
        <strong>❌ Ignoring Unknowns</strong>
        <p>If a ticket has research needed, add a spike first. Don''t estimate implementation when you don''t understand the problem.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <h3>The 90% Done Trap</h3>
    <div class="warn">
      <strong>⚠ Warning:</strong> "The first 90% of the work takes 90% of the time. The remaining 10% takes the other 90% of the time." — Tom Cargill
    </div>
    <p>This usually happens when estimates don''t include:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Error handling and edge cases</li>
      <li>Cross-browser / cross-device testing</li>
      <li>Code review iterations</li>
      <li>Documentation updates</li>
      <li>Migration and deployment considerations</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <!-- Quick Reference                                     -->
  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <div class="section" id="cheatsheet">
    <h2><span class="icon">📋</span> Quick Reference Card</h2>

    <h3>Sprint Planning Checklist</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Review team velocity (average of last 3 sprints)</li>
      <li>Account for planned absences, holidays, meetings</li>
      <li>Pull top-priority tickets from backlog</li>
      <li>Estimate each ticket using Planning Poker</li>
      <li>Set <code>story_points</code>, <code>level</code>, <code>dev_estimate_hours</code>, <code>tester_estimate_hours</code></li>
      <li>Assign tickets to team members based on level and expertise</li>
      <li>Verify total committed points ≤ team velocity</li>
      <li>Move tickets to sprint: <code>dt ticket move &lt;ID&gt; to sprint &lt;SID&gt;</code></li>
    </ol>

    <h3>After Completing a Ticket</h3>
    <pre><code># 1. Record actual time spent
$ dt ticket update 42 actual_hours "1d 4h"

# 2. Mark as done
$ dt ticket close 42</code></pre>

    <h3>CLI Estimation Commands</h3>
    <pre><code># Set story points
$ dt ticket update 42 points 5

# Update with multiple fields
$ dt ticket update 42 points 5 level moderate

# The estimated_by field is set automatically when you update via the API</code></pre>
  </div>

  <div class="footer">
    DevTeam Hub — Ticket Estimation Guide — May 2026
  </div>
</div>
</body>
</html>
