    <section id="why" class="section">
      <header>
        <h2>Why We Need Automatic Testing</h2>
        <div class="sub">Business reason: lower release risk, faster delivery, fewer regressions, better observability.</div>
      </header>
      <div class="body grid g-2">
        <div class="card">
          <h3>Current operational risks without broad automation</h3>
          <p>
            Manual checks scale poorly as project count grows. Defect discovery shifts late in the cycle, and environment-specific issues
            (especially in on-prem integrations) are detected too late.
          </p>
          <div class="warn">
            Late bug discovery multiplies cost. In practical delivery economics, fixing a bug in production can cost an order of magnitude more
            than catching it in PR/CI.
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="chart-wrap">
          <canvas id="riskReductionChart"></canvas>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="fit" class="section">
      <header>
        <h2>Best-Fit Tools For Our On-Prem Stack</h2>
        <div class="sub">Chosen tools prioritize self-hosting, predictable execution, and strong CI integration.</div>
      </header>
      <div class="body">
        <table class="table">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Layer</th>
              <th>Recommended Tooling</th>
              <th>Why It Fits On-Prem</th>
              <th>How We Use It</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td>BDD</td>
              <td>Cucumber (Ruby/JS), SpecFlow (.NET), Gherkin shared style guide</td>
              <td>No SaaS dependency, pure code + runner, easy in Jenkins</td>
              <td>Feature files become the source of truth for acceptance criteria.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Unit + Service/API</td>
              <td>RSpec (Rails), bun:test (Bun), xUnit/NUnit (.NET)</td>
              <td>Fast local + CI execution, stable ecosystems</td>
              <td>Every merge request runs deterministic test packs before integration.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>UI E2E</td>
              <td>Playwright</td>
              <td>Self-hosted runners, browser matrix support, trace/screenshot artifacts</td>
              <td>Critical journeys, smoke pack per deployment, nightly regression.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Reporting</td>
              <td>JUnit XML + Allure</td>
              <td>Artifact-based, easy to publish from Jenkins</td>
              <td>Dashboards: pass/fail, flaky tests, trend by suite/project.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Performance</td>
              <td>k6 + Grafana (self-hosted)</td>
              <td>Runs fully inside internal network</td>
              <td>Load and stress profiles before release and nightly baseline checks.</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="bdd" class="section">
      <header>
        <h2>BDD First: Gherkin As Shared Contract</h2>
        <div class="sub">BDD aligns product intent with executable tests and creates traceability from story to production checks.</div>
      </header>
      <div class="body grid g-2">
        <div class="card">
          <h3>Core process</h3>
          <p>
            1) Write acceptance criteria in Gherkin.<br>
            2) Implement steps against API/service boundaries.<br>
            3) Reuse scenarios for regression gates in Jenkins.<br>
            4) Link failing scenario back to ticket/project dashboard.
          </p>
          <div class="pill">Given/When/Then</div>
          <div class="pill">Business readable</div>
          <div class="pill">Executable in CI</div>
        </div>
