  [1m[36mDocument Create (1.1ms)[0m  [1m[32mINSERT INTO "documents" ("author_id", "content", "created_at", "doc_type", "is_template", "project_id", "summary", "template_id", "title", "updated_at", "version_number") VALUES (1, '# Jenkins CI — Solution Architecture & Setup Guide

## Overview

This document describes how the DevTeam uses Jenkins as the Continuous Integration
platform across all active projects. It covers the overall architecture, required
plugins, per-project `Jenkinsfile` configurations, and the Windows Server installation
procedure.

Jenkins is installed on a dedicated Windows Server. Each project defines its pipeline
in a `Jenkinsfile` at the repository root. Jenkins detects changes via GitHub webhooks
and reports build results back to DevTeam Hub through the `/webhooks/jenkins` endpoint,
which updates CI run records and broadcasts live status updates over Turbo Streams.

---

## 1. CI Flow

```
Developer pushes branch
        ↓
GitHub webhook triggers Jenkins
        ↓
Jenkins: checkout → restore deps → lint → test → build artifact
        ↓
Artifact archived (installers / dist / publish/)
        ↓
Deploy to staging  (main branch only)
        ↓
POST /webhooks/jenkins  →  DevTeam Hub updates CI run record
        ↓
Slack notification to #ci-notifications
```

---

## 2. Installing Jenkins on Windows Server

### Prerequisites

| Requirement | Minimum |
|-------------|---------|
| OS | Windows Server 2019 or 2022 |
| CPU | 4 cores |
| RAM | 8 GB (4 GB minimum) |
| Disk | 100 GB (builds accumulate quickly) |
| Java | Java 17 LTS (Eclipse Temurin) |
| Ports | 8080 (HTTP), 50000 (inbound agent) |

### Step 1 — Install Java 17

```powershell
winget install EclipseAdoptium.Temurin.17.JDK

# Persist JAVA_HOME for the machine
[System.Environment]::SetEnvironmentVariable(
    "JAVA_HOME",
    "C:\Program Files\Eclipse Adoptium\jdk-17",
    "Machine"
)
$env:PATH += ";$env:JAVA_HOME\bin"

java -version   # verify
```

### Step 2 — Download and Install Jenkins

```powershell
# Download the latest stable Windows installer
Invoke-WebRequest -Uri "https://get.jenkins.io/windows/latest" -OutFile "jenkins.msi"

# Silent install — registers Jenkins as a Windows Service automatically
Start-Process msiexec.exe -ArgumentList "/i jenkins.msi /quiet" -Wait

# Jenkins service starts automatically; check status
Get-Service -Name "Jenkins"
```

### Step 3 — Initial Setup Wizard

1. Open `http://localhost:8080` in a browser.
2. Retrieve the one-time admin password:
   ```powershell
   Get-Content "C:\ProgramData\Jenkins\.jenkins\secrets\initialAdminPassword"
   ```
3. Select **Install suggested plugins** — this installs Git, Pipeline, Blue Ocean, etc.
4. Install the additional plugins listed in Section 3.
5. Create the first admin user.
6. Set the **Jenkins URL** to your server hostname, e.g. `http://jenkins.devteam.local:8080`.

### Step 4 — Ensure Automatic Startup

```powershell
Set-Service -Name "Jenkins" -StartupType Automatic
Restart-Service -Name "Jenkins"
```

### Step 5 — Open Firewall Rules

```powershell
# Web UI
New-NetFirewallRule -DisplayName "Jenkins HTTP" -Direction Inbound `
    -Protocol TCP -LocalPort 8080 -Action Allow

# Jenkins agent JNLP port
New-NetFirewallRule -DisplayName "Jenkins Agent" -Direction Inbound `
    -Protocol TCP -LocalPort 50000 -Action Allow
```

### Step 6 — Install Build Toolchains

#### Ruby 3.4 (for Rails projects)

```powershell
winget install RubyInstallerTeam.RubyWithDevKit.3.4
# Bundler
gem install bundler
```

#### .NET SDK 8 (for TDI2 / WMS)

```powershell
winget install Microsoft.DotNet.SDK.8
dotnet --version   # verify
```

#### .NET Framework Build Tools (for Print Server / TDI)

```powershell
# Visual Studio Build Tools 2022 with .NET Framework workloads
winget install Microsoft.VisualStudio.2022.BuildTools
# Workloads to select: ".NET desktop build tools", "MSBuild"
```

#### Node.js 20 LTS (for Digital Internet Services)

```powershell
winget install OpenJS.NodeJS.LTS
node --version   # verify
```

#### NuGet CLI

```powershell
Invoke-WebRequest -Uri "https://dist.nuget.org/win-x86-commandline/latest/nuget.exe" `
    -OutFile "C:\tools\nuget.exe"
[System.Environment]::SetEnvironmentVariable("PATH", $env:PATH + ";C:\tools", "Machine")
```

### Step 7 — GitHub Integration

1. Create a GitHub Personal Access Token with scopes: `repo`, `admin:repo_hook`.
2. In Jenkins → **Manage Jenkins → Credentials → System → Global**:
   add a **Secret text** credential with ID `github-token`.
3. In **Manage Jenkins → System → GitHub**:
   add a GitHub Server pointing to `https://api.github.com` using `github-token`.
4. Jenkins now auto-creates webhooks on multi-branch pipeline jobs.

---

## 3. Required Plugins

Install via **Manage Jenkins → Plugins → Available plugins**:

| Plugin | Purpose |
|--------|---------|
| **Git** | Clone repositories |
| **GitHub** | Webhook trigger + commit status |
| **Pipeline** | Declarative `Jenkinsfile` support |
| **Blue Ocean** | Modern pipeline UI |
| **Multibranch Scan Webhook Trigger** | Trigger branch scans on push |
| **NodeJS** | Node.js tool auto-install |
| **MSBuild** | .NET Framework / MSBuild integration |
| **JUnit** | Parse test result XML |
| **HTML Publisher** | Publish coverage/test HTML reports |
| **HTTP Request** | POST webhook to DevTeam Hub |
| **Slack Notification** | Team Slack alerts |
| **Credentials Binding** | Inject secrets as env vars |
| **SSH Agent** | SSH key injection for deploys |
| **Timestamper** | Add timestamps to console output |
| **Workspace Cleanup** | `cleanWs()` step |
| **OWASP Dependency-Check** | CVE scanning |
| **Email Extension (Email-ext)** | Failure email notifications |
| **Configuration as Code (JCasC)** | Manage config in YAML |

---

## 4. Jenkins Configuration as Code (`jenkins.yaml`)

Place this file in `C:\ProgramData\Jenkins\.jenkins\` and set the
environment variable `CASC_JENKINS_CONFIG` to point to it.

```yaml
jenkins:
  systemMessage: "DevTeam CI — managed by JCasC"
  numExecutors: 4
  scmCheckoutRetryCount: 2
  globalNodeProperties:
    - envVars:
        env:
          - key: DEVTEAM_WEBHOOK_URL
            value: "http://devteam.local/webhooks/jenkins"
          - key: DEVTEAM_WEBHOOK_TOKEN
            value: "${DEVTEAM_WEBHOOK_TOKEN}"

credentials:
  system:
    domainCredentials:
      - credentials:
          - string:
              id: "github-token"
              description: "GitHub PAT"
              secret: "${GITHUB_TOKEN}"
          - string:
              id: "devteam-webhook-token"
              description: "DevTeam Hub webhook token"
              secret: "${DEVTEAM_WEBHOOK_TOKEN}"
          - usernamePassword:
              id: "nuget-server"
              username: "${NUGET_USER}"
              password: "${NUGET_PASS}"
              description: "Internal NuGet feed"

unclassified:
  slackNotifier:
    teamDomain: "devteam-workspace"
    tokenCredentialId: "slack-token"
    room: "#ci-notifications"
  gitHubPluginConfig:
    configs:
      - name: "GitHub"
        apiUrl: "https://api.github.com"
        credentialsId: "github-token"
        manageHooks: true
```

